      story: [
        "The Hot Club de France began as a club of Paris students who met to listen to records and argue about them; by 1932 it had a name, and within a few years it had concerts, a magazine and a band of its own, the Quintette du Hot Club de France with Django Reinhardt and Stéphane Grappelli [3]. Its two leaders, Hugues Panassié and Charles Delaunay, founded Jazz Hot in 1935, and in 1937 they added a record label, Disques Swing, with Delaunay and Panassié sharing the job of choosing artists and repertoire [2]. It was one of the first labels anywhere dedicated exclusively to jazz [2]. Delaunay, the son of the painters Robert and Sonia Delaunay, designed its logo, and the big French firm Pathé-Marconi pressed and distributed it until 1950 [1][2].",
        "The first session, in Paris in late April 1937, gathered the touring Coleman Hawkins, Benny Carter, Django Reinhardt, Stéphane Grappelli, the drummer Tommy Benford and the French saxophonist André Ekyan [1]. Its coupling of “Honeysuckle Rose” and “Crazy Rhythm” became Swing No. 1; the French Nouveau dictionnaire du jazz dates the session April 27, and some discographies give April 28 [1][4]. The early catalog mixed the Quintette (“Chicago”/“Charleston,” Swing 2) with visiting Americans such as Dicky Wells, Eddie South and Teddy Weatherford, and French bands such as Alix Combelle’s [4]. Delaunay, who also managed Reinhardt, recorded American visitors whenever they passed through [2].",
        "Under the German occupation Swing kept issuing French musicians playing American standards, renamed to slip past the censors: Combelle’s “Sweet Sue” came out as “Reflets” [1]. Delaunay, meanwhile, worked with the Resistance [2]. After the war the Hot Club split over bebop, which Delaunay championed and Panassié refused to call jazz; Delaunay was forced out in 1947 [3]. In 1951 he placed Swing inside Disques Vogue, the company he had co-founded, and the name survived there as an imprint — Thelonious Monk’s 1954 Paris solo LP carries both labels [1][5].",
      ],
      sound: "Swing was a collectors’ label made by collectors. Delaunay’s Hot Discography (1936) — the first jazz discography, later revised in five editions in England, France and the United States — listed sessions, dates and personnel, and Swing’s own records were built the same way, as documented jam sessions that put Americans and Europeans side by side [2][1].",
      significance: "Swing showed that fans could become producers. A club of amateurs, with a distribution deal with Pathé-Marconi, chose whom to record, wrote the history and published the reference books [1][2][3]. The Hot Club’s writers were also among the first to describe jazz as the creation of Black Americans; that conviction later hardened, in Panassié’s camp, into a rule about what counted as “authentic” [3].",
