        "SABA was a manufacturer based in Villingen, in the Black Forest. Hans Georg Brunner-Schwer, whose mother was the company’s heiress, became its technical director in 1961 and from 1963 produced records under a SABA label — jazz by Hans Koller, Wolfgang Dauner and Attila Zoller — while also distributing American jazz in West Germany [2]. He invited pianists, Oscar Peterson among them, to private house concerts and recorded them; for contractual reasons the Peterson tapes could not be released until 1968 [2][3].",
        "About 1968 the family sold most of SABA to the American conglomerate GTE. Brunner-Schwer bought back his productions and founded MPS — Musik Produktion Schwarzwald — on the old SABA site [2][1]. It was the first German label to release only jazz [1]. The critic Joachim-Ernst Berendt was among its producers; his “Jazz Meets the World” series paired jazz musicians with players from Bali, India and Tunisia [4]. The roster ran from Peterson and the Kenny Clarke–Francy Boland Big Band to Albert Mangelsdorff, Lee Konitz, Jean-Luc Ponty, George Duke and the Singers Unlimited, and MPS also issued concerts from the Berlin, Donaueschingen and Baden-Baden festivals [1].",
