      lead: "Two Munich fans with borrowed money started Enja in 1971; by recording Americans, Europeans and Japanese musicians side by side and selling licenses to Japan, they built one of the main archives of 1970s jazz.",
      story: [
        "Matthias Winckelmann and Horst Weber founded Enja — short for “European New Jazz” — in Munich in 1971, with borrowed money [2]. Their first notable recording was the pianist Mal Waldron, taped at Munich’s Domicile club on June 29, 1971, as Black Glory; the Waldron recording also let them sell licenses in Japan [2][3]. The early catalog mixed European and Japanese avant-gardists — Alexander von Schlippenbach, Albert Mangelsdorff, Terumasa Hino, Masahiko Sato, Yōsuke Yamashita — with Americans such as Archie Shepp, Cecil Taylor, Leroy Jenkins, Tommy Flanagan, Elvin Jones, Chet Baker, plus archival Eric Dolphy and Charles Mingus [1][2][4].",
        "In 1986 the founders split the catalog, then about 350 titles, into two independent branches [2]. Winckelmann’s Enja moved toward world jazz — Abdullah Ibrahim, Rabih Abou-Khalil, Turkish and Indonesian musicians — and added a classical imprint and a booking agency; Weber’s branch passed to Werner Aldinger, who added the Yellowbird imprint [1][2]. By 2012 the combined catalog was around 800 titles, and albums from it by Archie Shepp, Mal Waldron, Aki Takase and others had won the German record critics’ prize [2].",
      ],
      significance: "Enja is a clear case of a European label acting as an archive for American musicians in the 1970s, its early finances helped by licenses sold to Japan. Its catalog is international by design: the name promised European jazz, and the list soon covered three continents [1][2].",
