      story: [
        "FMP grew out of the New Artists Guild, an informal musicians’ cooperative of the mid-1960s. In 1968 the Guild presented the Total Music Meeting, a festival of music different from that heard at the official Berliner Jazztage; the name Free Music Production was adopted the next year, and the group began, as one reference account puts it, “operating as a cooperative venture under the administrative guidance of a former double bass player, Jost Gebers” [1]. Its founders were the saxophonist Peter Brötzmann, the bassist Peter Kowald, the pianist Alexander von Schlippenbach and Gebers [2]. The first release was Manfred Schoof’s European Echoes; Brötzmann’s ferocious octet album Machine Gun, recorded in May 1968 and first issued on his own BRÖ label, was reissued by FMP in the early 1970s [1][3][4].",
        "The collective dissolved in 1976 and Gebers ran the label alone [2]. Much of the catalog documented concerts, many of them organized by FMP itself [1]. From 1973 it released musicians from East Germany: the Petrowsky Quartett’s Just for Fun (1973) was the first recording by jazz musicians from both sides of the Wall [2][5]. In 1988 FMP recorded Cecil Taylor in Berlin and issued the results as the 11-CD Cecil Taylor in Berlin ’88, part of about twenty Taylor albums made between 1988 and 1992 [1][2].",
        "After 2000 FMP’s ownership was disputed; production paused in 2004, resumed in 2008 and ended in early 2011 [2][1]. In its first forty years it released “over 200 LPs and around 140 CDs” [1]. Critics honored the whole catalog with a Deutscher Kritikerpreis in 1977 and a Preis der deutschen Schallplattenkritik in 1990 [2]. Labels such as Atavistic, Intakt and JazzWerkstatt have reissued the catalog, and Munich’s Haus der Kunst staged an exhibition on FMP in 2017 [2].",
      ],
      significance: "FMP was musicians taking control of the whole chain — festival, recording, pressing, distribution. It gave European free improvisers a platform independent of record companies and state broadcasters, and in the divided city it became a channel between East and West German jazz [1][2][5].",
