        "Incus was founded in London in 1970 by the guitarist Derek Bailey, the saxophonist Evan Parker, the drummer Tony Oxley and the journalist Michael Walters [1][2]. After a seven-inch “Incus 0” of the group AMM came Incus 1, The Topography of the Lungs, recorded on July 13, 1970, by Parker, Bailey and the Dutch drummer Han Bennink — a landmark of English free improvisation [1][2]. An early letter set out the terms: most revenue would go to the musicians as royalties, and Incus did not intend to make a profit in the conventional sense [2]. Bailey’s Solo Guitar followed as Incus 2 [3].",
