      story: [
        "Giacomo Pellicciotti founded Black Saint in 1975 to record avant-garde musicians who might not otherwise have had the chance [1][2]. Its first release was the saxophonist Billy Harper’s album Black Saint (BSR 0001) [1][4]. The company was based in Tribiano, about half an hour southeast of Milan, and many sessions were made in Milan as touring Americans passed through [1][2]. In 1977 Hi-Fi & Record Center, a firm of which Giovanni Bonandrini was partner and president, took over Black Saint, which then had thirteen records in its catalog [3].",
        "In 1979 Bonandrini founded a sister label, Soul Note, because, he said, Black Saint’s producer wanted to record only the avant-garde and he felt the company should present “a larger view” [3]. Black Saint recorded Old and New Dreams, David Murray, Julius Hemphill, Oliver Lake, Muhal Richard Abrams, Henry Threadgill, Hamiet Bluiett, the World Saxophone Quartet, Anthony Braxton and Sun Ra, many of them members of Chicago’s AACM or St. Louis’s Black Artists Group; Soul Note recorded Archie Shepp, Max Roach, George Russell, Cecil Taylor, Steve Lacy, Charlie Haden and Mal Waldron [3][1].",
        "Bonandrini described the work as “simply a mission” and contrasted his labels with majors “for which jazz is only business” [3]. The numbers were tight: with more than 500 titles, all kept in print, he had cut new releases to twelve to fifteen a year “in order to stay in business,” and sales were split about 45 percent in Europe, 40 percent in North America and 15 percent in the Far East [3]. His son Flavio ran the labels until their sale to CAM Jazz in 2008 [1][2].",
      ],
      significance: "Black Saint and Soul Note are the clearest example of European labels as archive. An Italian company, selling more than half its records outside North America, documented a generation of Black American composers — from the AACM and the Black Artists Group — who might otherwise have had few chances to record [1][3].",
