        "In 1947 the actor and singer Ernst Busch received permission from the Soviet Military Administration to set up a music publisher, Lied der Zeit (“Song of the Times”), which included the Amiga label [1]. In 1954 it passed to the state company VEB Deutsche Schallplatten Berlin, under the Ministry of Culture, which held a monopoly on East German record production; Amiga’s share was popular music — folk, jazz, pop, rock, Schlager, chanson and children’s records [1][2]. In 1979 the state’s Potsdam-Babelsberg plant could press about twelve million LPs a year [2].",
        "Jazz and blues made up roughly 15 percent of Amiga’s output [2]. Licensed Western records appeared in an Amiga Jazz series, chosen to fit the criteria of cultural policy, and Amiga also issued free jazz licensed from West Berlin’s FMP [2]. Local players recorded too. The saxophonist Ernst-Ludwig Petrowsky played at Montreux in 1968 with Ensemble Studio IV, described as the first East German band to play jazz regularly; his group Synopsis made an Amiga LP in 1974, the same year FMP issued his quartet in the West [3][4]. After reunification the label was sold to BMG in 1994 [1].",
