      significance: "Amiga shows jazz inside a planned economy. The state owned the presses and decided which Western licenses fitted its cultural policy, yet East German improvisers recorded both for Amiga and, via cross-border deals, for FMP — one of the few places where Cold War jazz traded across the Wall [2][3].",
