        "Melodiya was created on May 11, 1964, after a decree of the Council of Ministers put the country’s studios and pressing plants under the Ministry of Culture [2]. It recorded in Moscow, Leningrad, Riga, Vilnius, Tashkent, Alma-Ata and Tbilisi, pressed at Aprelevka and other plants, and until the late 1980s was the only organization in the USSR that mass-produced records [2]. By 1973 it issued some 1,200 records a year in 190–200 million copies, dominated by classical music, Soviet composers, theater and children’s stories [1]. It began licensing Western recordings in 1973, after the USSR joined the Universal Copyright Convention; jazz, pop and rock licenses grew only in the late 1980s [2].",
        "Jazz had a hard road under Soviet rule: condemned as “bourgeois” in the late-1940s anti-cosmopolitan campaigns, it revived in the Khrushchev Thaw, though officials stayed wary [3]. The Ganelin Trio — Vyacheslav Ganelin, Vladimir Tarasov and Vladimir Chekasin — was an ensemble of the Lithuanian Philharmonic from 1971 to 1987 [4]. Melodiya, which had a studio in Vilnius until 1990, released its first LP, Con anima (sources date it 1976–78), and Concerto grosso (1980) [2][5][6]. Much of the trio’s work, though, reached the West through Leo Records in London [7]. Melodiya was privatized in 2020; in late 2025 it announced it would resume record production [2].",
