        "Gilles Peterson, born in Caen, France, in 1964, built a career as a DJ and label man long before Brownswood: he started the Acid Jazz label in 1988, moved on to Talkin’ Loud, backed by Phonogram, presented a Worldwide show on BBC Radio 1 from 1998 and moved to BBC 6 Music in 2012 [1]. He founded Brownswood in 2006 [1][2]. Its roster has ranged from José James and Ghostpoet to the Cuban singer Daymé Arocena, Zara McFarlane and Yussef Kamaal [3]. In 2016 it issued Wisdom of Elders, the debut of Shabaka Hutchings’s band Shabaka and the Ancestors [4][5].",
        "On February 9, 2018, Brownswood released We Out Here, nine tracks by Maisha, Ezra Collective, Moses Boyd, Theon Cross, Nubya Garcia, Shabaka Hutchings, TriForce, Joe Armon-Jones and Kokoroko, with Hutchings as musical director [5][4]. It marked, in one account, “a breakout into wider public consciousness of the UK jazz scene” [4]. Several of these players, Hutchings and Garcia among them, came through the youth program Tomorrow’s Warriors [4][6]. Kokoroko’s “Abusey Junction” drew some 57 million YouTube views, and the band stayed with Brownswood for its 2019 EP and its 2022 debut album [7]. Peterson launched a We Out Here festival in 2019 [8], and the label later formed a joint venture with Warner Music UK, beginning with Yussef Dayes’s Black Classical Music [3].",
